<!--
  FlowCanvasPortAddDemo.rozie — BEHAVIORAL runtime reproduction for Phase 83's WR-03
  finding (RETE-09's one remaining `human_verification` item, 83-VERIFICATION.md).

  THE SCENARIO THIS EXISTS TO PROVE. `markIncompatibleSockets`/`clearIncompatibleSockets`
  dim a type-mismatched target socket for the duration of a connection drag
  (FlowCanvas.rozie:2153-2186). Separately, `reconcileNodesPass`'s `portsAdded` branch
  (`:2503-2552`) disposes and rebuilds a node's socket DOM whenever that node's TYPE
  gains a new port — which strips the `rozie-flow-socket--incompatible` class the
  currently-active drag had applied, because the rebuild is a fresh DOM element with no
  memory of the mark. Commit `df9505e4a` (WR-03) fixed this by tracking the active pick
  in a component-scope `activePick` record and re-invoking `markIncompatibleSockets`
  synchronously, in the SAME reconcile pass, right after the socket rebuild
  (`:2538-2552`). The fix was verified statically (source re-read, codegen clean,
  71/71 unit tests) but had no executable reproduction — no demo fixture supported
  driving a live port-schema mutation WHILE a connection drag was in flight. This demo
  is that fixture.

  WHY A KEYDOWN, NOT A BUTTON. The fix only matters while the pointer is DOWN — a
  `portsAdded` reconcile that lands with no gesture active has nothing to re-mark. A
  button click cannot fire mid-drag: releasing the mouse to hit a DOM button would end
  the gesture (and the mark) before the reconcile could ever be observed under test.
  Playwright's `page.keyboard.press(...)` dispatches a key event without touching the
  mouse buttons, so a window-level keydown listener is the only trigger that can land
  while `page.mouse.down()` is still held. This mirrors D-14's own reasoning for why
  the Escape abort listener lives on `window`, not on the canvas container
  (FlowCanvas.rozie:1637-1639: rete's own pointer drag is driven from `window`, not a
  focused element) — the same discipline is followed here: `window.addEventListener`
  in `$onMount`, detached via the `$onMount`-returned teardown. The trigger key is `p`
  (for "port") — NOT `Escape`, which FlowCanvas's own D-14 listener already consumes to
  CLEAR the mark; a colliding key would make this fixture untestable.

  THE GRAPH — modelled directly on `FlowCanvasValidateOffDemo`'s typed pipeline: `src`
  (type `source`) declares a `number` and a `string` OUTPUT port; `mrg` (type `merge`)
  declares a `number` and a `string` INPUT port, both `multiple` (so a drag onto them
  never evicts a prior edge — irrelevant here since the graph never connects, but kept
  for parity with the family's typed-pipeline convention). A drag from `src`'s `number`
  output toward `mrg`'s `string` input is a genuine type mismatch, so the target socket
  dims mid-gesture exactly like `rete-flow-incompatible-socket` already proves.

  THE REACTIVE PORT. `mrg`'s `<NodeType>` carries a THIRD, conditionally-rendered
  `<Port r-if="$data.portAdded" input="extra" ...>` (wrapped in an explicit
  `<template #default>` — see the comment at the `merge` NodeType below for why).
  `<Port>` registers into its enclosing type's port schema on `$onMount` (Port.rozie)
  via the injected `addPort` verb, which lands in the canvas's `portReg` (keyed
  `type::side::key`) — and FlowCanvas's own `$watch(() => $data.portReg, ...)`
  (`:3416`) drives `reconcileNodes()` on ANY portReg change. So flipping
  `$data.portAdded` from false to true mounts the new `<Port>`, which registers a new
  INPUT on the `merge` TYPE, which fires the watch, which reconciles `mrg` (an
  EXISTING node) through the exact `portsAdded` branch WR-03 fixed — while the drag
  from step one is still paused with the pointer down and the `string` input still
  dimmed.

  `port-added-state` readout proves the toggle actually landed (not merely that the
  key was pressed) — the spec asserts it flips BEFORE checking the socket is still
  dimmed, so a silent no-op reconcile can't slip a false pass through.

  `:fit-on-mount="false"` keeps the seeded positions deterministic (the family
  convention); nodes sit far apart (40 / 420) so no socket overlaps. Zero connections
  — this fixture never needs to complete or reject a drag, only pause mid-gesture and
  observe the mark. NOT registered in any pixel-baseline matrix cell — this is
  transient mid-drag DOM state, the same reasoning `rete-flow-incompatible-socket`'s
  own header gives for staying screenshot-free (a mid-drag frame is gesture-timing-
  dependent by construction and per `feedback_vr_linux_baselines` every baseline PNG
  must be Linux-Docker-rendered, which makes a flaky baseline expensive to churn).

  Container needs explicit height/width (the VR host mounts at max-content).
-->

<rozie name="FlowCanvasPortAddDemo">

<components>
{
  FlowCanvas: '../../packages/ui/rete/src/FlowCanvas.rozie',
  NodeType:   '../../packages/ui/rete/src/NodeType.rozie',
  Port:       '../../packages/ui/rete/src/Port.rozie',
}
</components>

<data>
{
  // flips true on the 'p' keydown (window-level, mid-drag-capable — see header) and
  // never resets; drives the conditionally-rendered third <Port> below. Read by the
  // `port-added-state` readout so the spec can prove the toggle actually landed.
  portAdded: false,
  // THE SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH. Zero seeded connections — this fixture only pauses a
  // drag mid-gesture, it never needs to complete or reject one. Nodes sit far apart
  // (40 / 420) so no socket overlaps interfere with the drag geometry.
  graph: {
    nodes: [
      { id: 'src', type: 'source', x: 40,  y: 40,  data: { label: 'Number Source' } },
      { id: 'mrg', type: 'merge',  x: 420, y: 40,  data: { label: 'Merge' } },
    ],
    connections: [],
  },
}
</data>

<script>
// window-level, NOT container-level: rete's own pointer drag is driven from `window`
// itself (usePointerListener), so a `p` press must reach this listener regardless of
// where DOM focus happens to be mid-gesture — the exact D-14 Escape-listener reasoning
// (FlowCanvas.rozie:1637-1639). `p` is deliberately NOT `Escape`, which FlowCanvas's
// own D-14 window listener already consumes to CLEAR the incompatible mark.
$onMount(() => {
  const onPortAddKeydown = (e) => {
    if (e.key === 'p' && !$data.portAdded) {
      $data.portAdded = true
    }
  }
  window.addEventListener('keydown', onPortAddKeydown)
  // detach on unmount — the component's own D-14 abort listeners are the precedent
  // for this discipline (FlowCanvas.rozie:3344-3347).
  return () => window.removeEventListener('keydown', onPortAddKeydown)
})
</script>

<template>
<div class="flow-demo">
  <h3>FlowCanvas — mid-drag port-add keeps a marked socket dimmed (WR-03 regression gate)</h3>

  <div class="controls">
    <span class="readout" data-testid="port-added-state">{{ String($data.portAdded) }}</span>
    <span class="readout" data-testid="node-count">{{ $data.graph.nodes.length }}</span>
    <span class="readout" data-testid="conn-count">{{ $data.graph.connections.length }}</span>
  </div>

  <div class="flow-wrap" style="height: 360px; width: 760px">
    <FlowCanvas
      ref="canvas"
      r-model:graph="$data.graph"
      :fit-on-mount="false"
    >
      <!-- source: ONE type, BOTH a number AND a string OUTPUT port. -->
      <NodeType type="source">
        <template #body="{ node }">
          <div class="rozie-demo-node">
            <strong>{{ node.data.label }}</strong>
          </div>
        </template>
        <Port output="num" type="number" label="number" />
        <Port output="str" type="string" label="string" />
      </NodeType>

      <!-- merge: a number + string INPUT port (both `multiple`), PLUS a third INPUT
           port that only registers once $data.portAdded flips true — the live
           port-schema mutation this whole fixture exists to drive.

           The three <Port> children are wrapped in an EXPLICIT <template #default>
           (rather than left as bare loose children) — a compiler-lowering defect
           (independently reproduced against packages/core in isolation, unrelated to
           FlowCanvas/NodeType/Port) crashes `extractSlotFillers` with "Cannot read
           properties of undefined (reading 'type')" whenever a component tag carries
           a bare `r-if`-conditional loose child alongside a named `<template #name>`
           sibling fill (or even alone) — `lowerNodeList`'s r-if/r-else grouping
           lookahead desyncs the parallel componentChildren/loweredChildren index
           extractSlotFillers relies on. Wrapping the conditional (and its siblings)
           in an explicit `<template #default>` routes through the SAME `<slot />`
           NodeType.rozie renders for loose children (NodeType.rozie's template has no
           special-cases for `#body` vs `#default` vs implicit-loose — all three reach
           the same outlet), sidestepping the desync entirely with zero core changes.
           Scope fence: this fixture may not edit packages/core, so the compiler bug
           is worked around here, not patched at the source. -->
      <NodeType type="merge">
        <template #body="{ node }">
          <div class="rozie-demo-node">
            <strong>{{ node.data.label }}</strong>
          </div>
        </template>
        <template #default>
          <Port input="num" type="number" label="number" multiple />
          <Port input="str" type="string" label="string" multiple />
          <Port r-if="$data.portAdded" input="extra" type="bool" label="extra" multiple />
        </template>
      </NodeType>
    </FlowCanvas>
  </div>
</div>
</template>

<style>
.flow-demo {
  font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif;
  color: #1a1a1a;
  padding: 1rem;
  max-width: 800px;
}
.flow-demo h3 { margin: 0 0 0.75rem; font-size: 1rem; }
.controls {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 0.5rem;
  margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
}
.controls .readout {
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  font-size: 0.875rem;
  min-width: 2ch;
}

:root {
  /* the #body chrome teleports into the engine node element inside the canvas shadow
     root, so on Lit a plain scoped rule cannot reach it — :root emits document-level
     CSS adopted into the shadow root. NOT :global() (ROZ128 hard error). */
  .rozie-flow-canvas .rozie-demo-node {
    padding: 0.5rem 0.75rem;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: #111827;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
}
</style>

</rozie>
